  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1226 PM MDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nQUIET SATURDAY:\n\nToday will see eastern Utah and western Colorado under something of \na dry slot, sandwiched between yesterday's shortwave and the \napproaching longer wave trough. Dry is a bit of a misnomer, though, \nas PWATs remain around 150% of normal today, resulting in widespread \ncloud cover and a few stray orographic showers through the \nafternoon. The upper level southwesterly jet noses in after 6PM this \nevening, bringing an increasing chance of showers and a few \nthunderstorms to areas around the Four Corners. Any activity at this \ntime is expected to be isolated to widely scattered, and trick or \ntreating for much of the Western Slope will be in the dry. \n\n\nLOWER ELEVATION RAIN, MOUNTAIN SNOW, AND FLOODING POTENTIAL:\n\nPrecipitation begins to fill in from southwest to northeast after \nmidnight tonight, becoming widespread by daybreak Sunday. All this \nprecipitation is being fueled by a robust AR that is taking a very \nfavorable trajectory up through southern California and setting its \nsights on the Four Corners region. PWAT anomalies in both the \nEnsemble ECMWF and Ensemble GFS are on the order of 250-350% of \nnormal for this time of year. This equates to between 0.75 inches \nand 1.00 inches of precipitable water. For comparison, normal for \nthis time of year is 0.30-0.40 inches of precipitable water. This is \nan exceptionally juicy airmass, and as is often the case with \nairmasses of tropical origin like these, it will also be a warm one. \nSnow levels are expected to hover between 10k and 12k feet through \nall of Sunday and into Sunday evening, before finally beginning to \nlower to 7000-9000 feet late Sunday night into Monday. What this \nmeans, ultimately, is that a majority of locations will see this \nprecipitation fall as rain, with snow relegated to areas above 10k \nfeet. The San Juans are looking at 1-3 inches of QPE over a 24-36 \nhour period, with locally higher amounts up to 4 inches possible. \nElsewhere, lower elevation valleys could see 0.50-1.00 inches, with \nhigher terrain in the 1-2 inch range during the same period. With \nalready very saturated soils, periods of heavy rain could result in \nflash flooding and debris flow issues, especially for sensitive \nareas. Additionally, rivers are expected respond to all of this \nliquid precipitation, with some of the same areas hit hard last week \nlooking at another round of river flooding. A Flood Watch is in \neffect for all of southeast Utah and southwest Colorado, with \nseveral targeted River Flood Warnings in effect across the same \narea. \n\nWhile snow is confined mainly to areas above 10k feet, amounts are \nnothing to sneeze at. For the San Juans, snow totals by Monday \nevening are on the order of 12-18 inches, with locally higher \namounts up to 2 feet possible. For the central Divide mountains, the \nGrand Mesa, and the Flat Tops, amounts are less, in the 5-10 inch \nrange, with locally higher amounts around 1 foot possible. As a \nresult, a Winter Storm Warning is in effect for the San Juans, with \nWinter Weather Advisories in effect for the central Divide \nmountains, Grand Mesa, and Flat Tops. As snow levels lower late \nSunday night into Monday morning, some of this snow will reach pass \nlevel. Be prepared for sloppy conditions during the Monday morning \ncommute along US-550, US-50, and along I-70 over Vail Pass. \n\nNEXT WEEK:\n\nBehind this system, conditions dry out for a few days as high \namplitude ridging moves overhead. We'll see a slight warm up through \nthe mid-week period, although highs remain 5-10 degrees below \nnormal for early November. Looking further out, a quick moving \nclipper system has the potential to bring some light showers to the \nregion mid-to-late next week, although model agreement is poor.",
